concepts.body:
A **slice** `&s[a..b]` is a view of the bytes from `a` up to (not including) `b`, with no copying:

```rust
let line = "gold: 30";
let name = &line[0..4];    // "gold"
let rest = &line[6..];     // "30" (to the end)
let head = &line[..4];     // "gold" (from the start)
```

The numbers are **byte** positions, and they must fall on a character boundary. Otherwise the program **panics** at runtime:

```rust
let name = "Zoë";
let bad = &name[0..3];   // panic: byte index 3 is not a char boundary
```

That is also why `s[0]` doesn't compile (E0277: `str` cannot be indexed by an integer): "the 0th byte" might be half a character. In practice you rarely compute byte positions yourself. Let `find`, `split`, `split_once` or `chars` find the positions, and they are always on a boundary.

Start with an empty `mut String` and append to it:

```rust
let mut out = String::new();
for word in text.split_whitespace() {
    if !out.is_empty() {
        out.push(' ');          // a separator between, not after
    }
    out.push_str(word);
}
```

Or append whole formatted lines with `out.push_str(&format!("{n}. {word}\n"));`.

Programs in the tutor can read their input (the **stdin** box next to Run, and the hidden test cases) with this recipe:

```rust
use std::io::{self, Read};

fn main() {
    let mut text = String::new();
    io::stdin().read_to_string(&mut text).unwrap();
    for line in text.lines() {
        // …
    }
}
```

`&mut text` lets `read_to_string` write into your `String` (Level 2), and `.unwrap()` panics if reading fails (Level 5). Until then, copy the recipe as it is.

